(   - Affect \(Almost Always a Verb\): To exert an influence upon, alter, or produce a change in) Tj
T*
(   something:) Tj
T*
(   - "Substrate concentration affects the initial reaction velocity.") Tj
T*
(   - Effect \(Almost Always a Noun\): The concrete outcome, result, or consequence brought about by) Tj
T*
(   a cause:) Tj
T*
(   - "The allosteric inhibitor had a profound effect on enzyme kinetics.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Rare Reverse Grammatical Usages in High-Register Prose:) Tj
T*
(   - Effect as a Verb \(Meaning 'to bring about / to cause to exist / to execute'\):) Tj
T*
(   - Used in formal institutional, managerial, or biochemical contexts:) Tj
T*
(   - "The gene therapy effected a dramatic transformation in patient survival.") Tj
T*
(   - "The committee effected major policy revisions." \(Notice: means brought into reality\).) Tj
T*
(   - Affect as a Noun \(Pronounced with initial stress /'ae.fekt/\):) Tj
T*
(   - Strictly a clinical psychiatry / psychology term denoting external emotional expression:) Tj
T*
(   - "The clinical trial participant exhibited a flat affect during neurological evaluation.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Mnemonic Diagnostic:) Tj
T*
(   Remember RAVEN: Remember Affect is a Verb, Effect is a Noun.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
